  "worked_solution": "When the circle intercepts the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (0+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y+2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the circle does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/86f198b30096cf3106186ee2595b61c21be000e8e6223c5b42ee453ba1b9fe4a.png)",
